This is the script repository for 'The entropy of resting-state neural dynamics is a marker of general cognitive ability in childhood' (Zdorovtsova et al.).

The repo is split into two parts:

- **MATLAB** owns preprocessing, HMM inference, and spectral decomposition - everything
  that depends on the OSL/HMM-MAR/SPM/Workbench toolboxes and has no viable Python
  equivalent.
- **Python** (the `analysis/` package) owns everything downstream of that: the
  statistics run on the HMM's summary outputs, multiplicity correction, and figures.
  This is where the resubmission's statistical redesign lives.

## MATLAB: preprocessing, HMM inference, spectral decomposition

Dependencies:
-SPM 12
-The OSL repository (https://github.com/OHBA-analysis/osl/) and more specifically, the HMM-MAR toolbox (https://github.com/OHBA-analysis/HMM-MAR)
-HCP Workbench (for visualisation; see https://humanconnectome.org/software/connectome-workbench)

The order in which you do MEG data preprocessing is important. We did things in this order, with these scripts and programs:

1. Maxfiltering and ICA artefact removal (see Python script 'HMM_MaxfilterAndICA.py').
2. Conversion into .mat and .dat files (SPM 12)
3. Bandpass filtering between 1Hz and 30Hz (SPM 12)
4. Coregistration to T1 scans (SPM 12)
5. Beamforming (see Matlab script 'HMM_Beamforming.m').
6. Parcellation (see Matlab script 'HMM_ApplyParcellation_38.m').
7. Other preprocessing - including detrending, channel standardisation, leakage correction, application of a Hilbert envelope, and downsampling (see Matlab script 'HMM_FinishPreprocessing.m').

You can look for bad segments in your timeseries data (and exclude participants as necessary) using 'HMM_BadSegments.m'.

HMM computation is done within the matlab script 'HMM_Compute.m'.

Spectral estimation and decomposition across HMM states is done in 'HMM_Spectra.m'.

Finally, you can visualise HMM states in HCP Workbench using 'HMM_Visualise.m'.

Once the HMM has been fit, run **'HMM_ExportForPython.m'** to write the three tidy files the
Python package consumes: `subject_level.csv`, `state_level.csv`, and
`transition_matrices.csv` (one row per subject; one row per subject x state; one row per
subject x from_state x to_state, respectively - see the docstring at the top of that script
for exact column definitions). This replaces the old scattered `xlsread`/`csvread`/`csvwrite`
calls that used to live inside the analysis scripts themselves.

## Python: statistics and figures (`analysis/`)

Everything downstream of the three exported CSVs - what used to be
`HMM_BetweenStatesAnalyses.m`, `HMM_CognitiveBehaviouralAnalyses.m`, and the statistical/plotting
half of `HMM_Entropy.m` - now lives in the `analysis/` Python package:

- `analysis/io.py` - loads and validates the three exported CSVs.
- `analysis/entropy.py` - stationary distribution and entropy rate from a transition
  matrix (a port of `HMM_Entropy.m`'s math, used to independently cross-check the
  entropy rate MATLAB already computed).
- `analysis/models.py` - mixed-effects models with state as a within-subject factor
  (replacing the old per-state GLM loops), plus residual-normality and VIF diagnostics
  run for every model.
- `analysis/correction.py` - one multiplicity correction across every test actually
  reported in the paper, replacing the old `multicmp.m` calls that only corrected
  within their own sub-family.
- `analysis/viz/` - shared plotting style (`style.py`) and one function per paper
  figure (`figures.py`).

Install with `pip install -r requirements.txt` (or `pip install -e .[dev]`), and run the
test suite with `pytest`.

We did not include modularity or communicability calculations across state transition
matrices in this paper, but if you're interested in those relationships, see the commented-out
cells at the bottom of the old `HMM_Entropy.m` history for a starting point - in a past
paper of ours (Zdorovtsova et al., 2023: https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cortex.2023.04.001), we
found that nodal communicability is related to cognitive ability in a sample of children with
developmental difficulties.

If you have any further questions, please reach out to the lead author, Natalia Zdorovtsova, at: natalia.zdorovtsova@mrc-cbu.cam.ac.uk
